    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="pill">Overview</span>
      <h2>A modern Taíno language</h2>
      <p><strong>Maburika</strong> (Welcome)! Ahiyaniwa is a modern revitalization of the Taíno language of the Greater Antilles. My name is <strong>Casey Alberto Roche</strong>, and I'm a Cuban American linguist with distant Taíno ancestry. I'm also a member of the Higuayagua Taino Tribe. My project is still young, but it grows a little more every day, and you are more than welcome to explore it!</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-3">
      <article class="card accent">
        <h3>The name</h3>
        <p><strong>Ahiyaniwa</strong> means “language.” It is built from <em>ahiya</em> “to speak” plus the nominalizing material <em>niwa</em>, which keeps the name centered on speech, community, and everyday use.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card coral">
        <h3>Arawakan roots</h3>
        <p>Classic Taíno is a Ta-Arawakan language, and Ahiyaniwa seeks to maintain this core. As such, it follows Arawakan patterns while thoughtfully simplifying for accessibility: pronominal prefixes, agglutinative verbs, postpositions, stative predication, and an open syllable aesthetic.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card gold">
        <h3>Authenticity first</h3>
        <p>There are a few Taíno reconstructions in circulation, but Ahiyaniwa is different. It strives to keep its <strong>Arawakan authenticity</strong> and reduce English/Spanish influences by working from colonial sources, historical linguistics, and close sister languages such as Lokono and Wayuu.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-2" style="margin-top: 20px;">
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Where the project stands</h3>
        <p>Ahiyaniwa is in its early stages. The dictionary is already substantial, the grammar is being standardized, and the lexicon keeps expanding. It is a work in progress, and it is worked on every day.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Orthography standard</h3>
        <p>This site uses a consistent modern spelling: accent marks on verb suffixes, tildes for nasal vowels, and an apostrophe between a prefix and a vowel initial root.</p>
        <div class="orthography">
          <span>Verb suffixes: ká, bá, bó, há, té</span>
          <span>Nasal vowels: ã, ẽ, ĩ, õ, ũ</span>
          <span>Elision: d'ahiya, b'ata, w'okona</span>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
