  <!-- =========================================================
       MISSION
       ========================================================= -->
  <section id="mission" class="page">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="pill coral">Mission</span>
      <h2>Reconnecting a Caribbean language</h2>
      <p>For a long time, conventional wisdom said Taíno was completely gone, leaving behind only loanwords such as <em>hamaka</em> “hammock,” <em>hurakã</em> “hurricane,” <em>kanowa</em> “canoe,” and <em>tabako</em> “tobacco.” Ahiyaniwa refuses to let the language stay a footnote in history.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-2">
      <article class="card accent">
        <h3>Why this project exists</h3>
        <p>As a Taíno descendant, member of the Higuayagua Taino Tribe, and linguist, this work is deeply personal. I am dedicated to embracing and promoting Taíno language and culture. Work continues every day: refining the lexicon, testing grammatical patterns, standardizing spelling, and expanding the available materials.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card coral">
        <h3>Arawakan authenticity</h3>
        <p>A few Taíno reconstructions exist, but Ahiyaniwa is built to stay close to Arawakan structure. It does not invent forms out of thin air. It follows the linguistic breadcrumbs left by sixteenth century sources, historical linguists, and sister languages.</p>
        <p>The goal is not merely to make “Taíno sounding” words. The goal is a usable language with authentic Arawakan morphology, syntax, and phonological shape that learners can acquire quickly.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-2" style="margin-top: 20px;">
      <article class="card">
        <h3>From colonial sources</h3>
        <p>Chroniclers such as Fray Ramón Pané, Bartolomé de las Casas, and Gonzalo Fernández de Oviedo recorded Taíno words, place names, personal names, and short phrases. But they were not trained phoneticians. They wrote with sixteenth century Spanish spelling, which is why historical spellings like <em>Buren</em>, <em>Caiman</em>, and <em>Tiburon</em> have to be filtered into forms like <em>burẽ</em>, <em>kaimã</em>, and <em>tiburõ</em>.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>From sister languages</h3>
        <p>When the historical record runs out, Ahiyaniwa compares Taíno with Lokono, Wayuu, and reconstructed Proto-Lokono-Wayuu roots. Regular sound correspondences help bridge lexical gaps while respecting the open syllable rhythm, intervocalic lenition, and Arawakan grammatical skeleton of Taíno.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="note">
      <strong>Mission statement:</strong> Ahiyaniwa is a labor of love. It is an always expanding, community-centered reconstruction that honors the ancestors through Arawakan structure, Caribbean sound, and usability in the modern world.
    </div>
  </section>

