                <td><span class="ipa-empty">—</span></td>
                <td><span class="ipa-item"><span class="sym">s</span><span class="val">/s/</span></span></td>
                <td><span class="ipa-item"><span class="sym">x</span><span class="val">/ʃ/</span></span></td>
                <td><span class="ipa-empty">—</span></td>
                <td><span class="ipa-item"><span class="sym">h</span><span class="val">/h/</span></span></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Approximant</td>
                <td><span class="ipa-item"><span class="sym">w</span><span class="val">/w/</span></span></td>
                <td><span class="ipa-empty">—</span></td>
                <td><span class="ipa-item"><span class="sym">y</span><span class="val">/j/</span></span></td>
                <td><span class="ipa-empty">—</span></td>
                <td><span class="ipa-empty">—</span></td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="small" style="margin-bottom: 0;">Note: <em>x</em> represents the postalveolar fricative <span class="mono">/ʃ/</span>, and <em>w</em> is labio-velar <span class="mono">/w/</span>.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-2" style="margin-top: 20px;">
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Syllable structure</h3>
        <p>CV, V, and CVC are all possible. Any consonant can start a syllable, but the coda is highly restricted and consonant clusters are avoided. The language strongly favors open syllables and a smooth, vowel final rhythm.</p>
        <p class="small" style="margin-bottom: 0;">Template: <span class="mono" style="color: var(--deep); font-weight: 600;">CV, V, CVC</span></p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Phonological rules</h3>
        <p><strong>Allophony:</strong> <span class="mono">/d/</span> becomes <span class="mono">[ɾ]</span> between vowels. In Taíno writing, <em>d</em> occurs word initially, while <em>r</em> is used between vowels.</p>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0;"><strong>Stress:</strong> usually penultimate, but final if the word ends in <span class="mono">/i/</span>, the diphthong <span class="mono">/ei/</span>, or a nasal vowel. Vowel suffixes also make stress word final.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- =========================================================
       GRAMMAR
       ========================================================= -->
  <section id="grammar" class="page">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="pill">Grammar</span>
      <h2>Arawakan grammar, clearly organized</h2>
      <p>Ahiyaniwa is agglutinative and synthetic, which means it likes to pack a lot of meaning into single words. It uses bound morphemes for person, possession, tense, and aspect, and it prefers verb first word order. Do not worry if it looks like a lot at first. Once you see the patterns, it is remarkably regular. Let us walk through it together, one piece at a time.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="jump-bar" aria-label="Grammar sections">
      <a class="jump-link" href="#gram-pronouns">Pronouns &amp; Elision (1–4)</a>
      <a class="jump-link" href="#gram-nouns">Nouns &amp; Postpositions (5–7)</a>
      <a class="jump-link" href="#gram-verbs">Verbs &amp; Clauses (8–12)</a>
      <a class="jump-link" href="#gram-misc">Demonstratives &amp; Numbers (13–14)</a>
      <a class="jump-link" href="#gram-tobe">Deep Dive: “To Be”</a>
      <a class="jump-link" href="#gram-suffixes">Suffixes &amp; Postpositions</a>
    </div>

    <!-- GROUP 1 -->
    <div id="gram-pronouns" class="sub-section-head">
      <h3>Pronouns &amp; Morphophonology</h3>
      <span class="sub-section-tag">Rules 1 – 4</span>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-2">
      <article class="grammar-card">
        <div class="grammar-card-head">
          <span class="grammar-num">1</span>
          <h3>Pronominal prefixes</h3>
        </div>
        <p>These little prefixes are the building blocks of the language. Attach one to a <strong>noun</strong> and it shows possession (“my,” “your,” “his”). Attach one to a <strong>verb</strong> and it shows who is doing the action (“I,” “you,” “he”).</p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>Singular</th><th>Plural</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>1st</td><td><strong>da</strong> (my / I)</td><td><strong>wa</strong> (our / we)</td></tr>
              <tr><td>2nd</td><td><strong>bu</strong> (your / you)</td><td><strong>hu</strong> (your PL / you PL)</td></tr>
              <tr><td>3rd Masc.</td><td><strong>li</strong> (his / he)</td><td><strong>na</strong> (their / they)</td></tr>
              <tr><td>3rd Fem.</td><td><strong>tu</strong> (her / she)</td><td><strong>na</strong> (their / they)</td></tr>
              <tr><td>3rd Inan.</td><td><strong>to</strong> (its / it)</td><td><strong>na</strong> (their / they)</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="small" style="margin-bottom: 0;">Noun example: <em>d'ako</em> (da + ako) “my eye.” Verb example: <em>daká</em> (da + ká) “I am.”</p>
      </article>

      <article class="grammar-card">
        <div class="grammar-card-head">
          <span class="grammar-num">2</span>
          <h3>Independent emphatic pronouns</h3>
        </div>
        <p>Because the prefix on the verb already tells you who is doing the action, you do not strictly need a separate subject word. But when you want to emphasize the actor (“<em>I</em> did it myself”), answer a question with a standalone pronoun, or make a contrast, add the focus element <strong>kiya</strong> to the pronominal root.</p>
        <p><em>dakiya</em> “I / me / myself,” <em>bukiya</em> “you / yourself,” <em>likiya</em> “he / himself,” <em>tukiya</em> “she / herself,” <em>wakiya</em> “we / ourselves,” <em>hukiya</em> “you all / yourselves,” <em>nakiya</em> “they / themselves.” The inanimate pronoun remains simply <em>to</em>.</p>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">When used in a sentence, these usually go at the very front for emphasis, right before the prefixed verb: <em>wakiya w'ahiyaba</em> “we ourselves spoke.”</p>
      </article>

      <article class="grammar-card">
        <div class="grammar-card-head">
          <span class="grammar-num">3</span>
          <h3>Object pronouns</h3>
        </div>
        <p>When a pronoun is on the receiving end of an action (<em>me, you, him, her, us, them</em>), or attached to a directional ending like “to me” or “with us,” Ahiyaniwa uses special object forms. They are placed <strong>after</strong> the verb or attached directly to a postposition.</p>
        <p><em>de</em> “me,” <em>wo</em> “us,” <em>bu</em> “you,” <em>hu</em> “you all,” <em>li</em> “him,” <em>tu</em> “her,” <em>to</em> “it,” <em>nu</em> “them.”</p>
        <p class="small" style="margin-bottom: 0;">Examples: <em>bumũ</em> “to you,” <em>demũ</em> “for me,” <em>numũ</em> “to them.”</p>
      </article>

      <article class="grammar-card">
        <div class="grammar-card-head">
          <span class="grammar-num">4</span>
          <h3>The vowel drop rule (elision)</h3>
        </div>
        <p>Taíno loves smooth, open syllables, so it avoids awkward vowel collisions at prefix boundaries. Whenever a prefix ending in a vowel meets a root that starts with a vowel, <strong>drop the prefix vowel</strong>. This is often marked with an apostrophe in writing.</p>
        <p><em>da</em> (my) + <em>anixi</em> (heart) becomes <em>d'anixi</em> “my heart.”<br>
           <em>wa</em> (we) + <em>okona</em> (walk) + <em>ba</em> (past) becomes <em>w'okonaba</em> “we walked.”</p>
        <p class="small" style="margin-bottom: 0;">Elision is entirely regular. It applies wherever the prefix meets a vowel initial root, including before postpositions.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <!-- GROUP 2 -->
    <div id="gram-nouns" class="sub-section-head">
      <h3>Nouns, Plurals &amp; Postpositions</h3>
      <span class="sub-section-tag">Rules 5 – 7</span>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-3">
      <article class="grammar-card">
        <div class="grammar-card-head">
          <span class="grammar-num">5</span>
          <h3>Articles and determiners</h3>
        </div>
        <p>Ahiyaniwa uses both bound prefixes and free particles to mark definiteness.</p>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0;"><strong>ka</strong> is an attributive definite prefix for inanimate nouns (<em>kabohiyo</em> “the house”). <strong>li</strong> is a free article for masculine human nouns (<em>li kasike</em> “the chief”). <strong>to</strong> is the free article for feminine and inanimate nouns (<em>to hamaka</em> “the hammock”). Indefinite <strong>aba</strong> means “a” or “an,” and <strong>ababe</strong> or <strong>abano</strong> mean “some” or “several.”</p>
      </article>

      <article class="grammar-card">
        <div class="grammar-card-head">
          <span class="grammar-num">6</span>
          <h3>Pluralization</h3>
        </div>
        <p>Plural suffixes are common but not obligatory when the noun is already modified by a number word or a plural determiner.</p>
        <p><strong>no</strong> is used for animate and human nouns: <em>kasikeno</em> “chiefs,” <em>taino</em> “good people.” <strong>be</strong> is used for inanimate and general nouns: <em>arabe</em> “trees,” <em>anabe</em> “flowers.”</p>
        <p class="small" style="margin-bottom: 0;">You can say <em>biyã ara</em> “two trees” without adding <em>be</em>, since the numeral already marks the plural.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="grammar-card">
        <div class="grammar-card-head">
          <span class="grammar-num">7</span>
          <h3>Postpositions</h3>
        </div>
        <p>Unlike English, which puts prepositions <em>before</em> the noun, Ahiyaniwa puts its relation words <strong>after</strong> the noun. They are called postpositions, and they attach to the end of the noun or pronoun. Like prefixes, when attached to words ending in a vowel, they follow the vowel-drop (elision) rule. When a postposition that starts with a vowel follows a nasal vowel, an epenthetic consonant is inserted.</p>
        <p><strong>mũ</strong> “to / for,” <strong>oriya</strong> “from / of,” <strong>oma</strong> “with / in the company of,” <strong>abo</strong> “using / with,” <strong>yo</strong> “toward,” <strong>kowa</strong> “at / on,” <strong>oloko</strong> “inside.”</p>
        <p class="small" style="margin-bottom: 0;">Examples: <em>arabukoyo</em> “toward the forest,” <em>d'awõmabo</em> “with my dog,” <em>kabohiyo'loko</em> “inside the house.”</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <!-- GROUP 3 -->
    <div id="gram-verbs" class="sub-section-head">
      <h3>Verbs, Negation &amp; Clause Structure</h3>
      <span class="sub-section-tag">Rules 8 – 12</span>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-2">
      <article class="grammar-card">
        <div class="grammar-card-head">
          <span class="grammar-num">8</span>
          <h3>Verb morphology and tense</h3>
        </div>
        <p>Every active verb follows the same simple formula: <strong>pronoun prefix + verb root + tense or aspect suffix</strong>. The main suffixes are:</p>
        <p><strong>ká</strong> present, stative, or habitual, <strong>bá</strong> past or perfective, <strong>bó</strong> progressive, <strong>há</strong> future or irrealis, and <strong>té</strong> imperative.</p>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">With <em>ahiya</em> “to speak”: <em>b'ahiyabá</em> “you spoke,” <em>d'ahiyabó</em> “I am speaking,” <em>tuboriká</em> “she works,” <em>d'ahiyahá</em> “I will speak.”</p>
      </article>

      <article class="grammar-card">
        <div class="grammar-card-head">
          <span class="grammar-num">9</span>
          <h3>The copula “to be”</h3>
        </div>
        <p>Ahiyaniwa does not have a separate verb root for “to be.” Instead, the stative suffix <strong>ká</strong> attaches directly to the pronoun root: <em>daká</em> “I am,” <em>buká</em> “you are,” <em>liká</em> “he is,” <em>tuká</em> “she is,” <em>toká</em> “it is,” <em>waká</em> “we are,” <em>huká</em> “you all are,” <em>naká</em> “they are.”</p>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">To shift the tense, just swap the suffix: past <em>dabá</em> “I was,” future <em>dahá</em> “I will be,” progressive <em>dabó</em> “I am being.”</p>
      </article>

      <article class="grammar-card">
        <div class="grammar-card-head">
          <span class="grammar-num">10</span>
          <h3>Negation</h3>
        </div>
        <p>To say that something is <em>not</em> happening or <em>not</em> in a certain state, attach the negative prefix <strong>ma</strong> (“without” or “non occurrence”) to the front of the verb or stative root.</p>
        <p><em>maxaliká</em> “is not happy,” <em>matoxaliká</em> “it is not happy,” <em>mad'arikẽká d'itu</em> “I do not see my sister.”</p>
        <p class="small" style="margin-bottom: 0;">The negative prefix behaves just like any other prefix. It simply goes at the very front of the word.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="grammar-card">
        <div class="grammar-card-head">
          <span class="grammar-num">11</span>
          <h3>Imperatives (commands)</h3>
        </div>
        <p>Commands are formed by prefixing the person you are addressing and adding <strong>té</strong> to the verb root: <em>b'ahiyaté!</em> “Speak!” <em>hukãmaté!</em> “You all listen!”</p>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">One common exception: “let us go” is usually just <em>w'aibá!</em> rather than <em>w'aibate!</em></p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <article class="grammar-card" style="margin-top: 20px;">
      <div class="grammar-card-head">
        <span class="grammar-num">12</span>
        <h3>Word order and sentence building</h3>
      </div>
      <p>Ahiyaniwa prefers verb first order: <strong>V S O</strong> or <strong>V S Location</strong>. Even when a full noun subject appears, the verb at the beginning keeps its matching pronoun prefix.</p>
      <p><em>Toxaliká d'anixi kawayabakowa</em> “My heart is happy at the guava tree,” literally “It is happy, my heart, at the guava tree.”</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">Modifiers generally come before the noun they describe: <em>semiku wayaba</em> “sweet guava.”</p>
    </article>

    <!-- GROUP 4 -->
    <div id="gram-misc" class="sub-section-head">
      <h3>Demonstratives, Comparison &amp; Numbers</h3>
      <span class="sub-section-tag">Rules 13 – 14</span>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-2">
      <article class="grammar-card">
        <div class="grammar-card-head">
          <span class="grammar-num">13</span>
          <h3>Demonstratives</h3>
        </div>
        <p>“This” and “that” agree with the noun's gender class. For masculine human nouns: <em>lihi</em> and <em>lihino</em> “this” and “these,” <em>lira</em> and <em>lirano</em> “that” and “those.” For feminine human nouns: <em>tuhi</em> and <em>tuhino</em>, <em>tura</em> and <em>turano</em>. For inanimates: <em>toho</em> and <em>tohobe</em>, <em>tora</em> and <em>torabe</em>.</p>
        <p class="small" style="margin-bottom: 0;">Example: <em>toho bohiyo</em> “this house,” <em>torabe arabuko</em> “those forests.”</p>
      </article>

      <article class="grammar-card">
        <div class="grammar-card-head">
          <span class="grammar-num">14</span>
          <h3>Comparatives and numbers</h3>
        </div>
        <p>The comparative suffix <strong>ha</strong> means “er”: <em>tuyuha</em> “taller.” The standalone word <em>sabo</em> “more” can also be used, often with the ablative <em>oriya</em>: <em>liká li kasike sabo tuyu li ir'oriya</em> “the chief is taller than the man.”</p>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">The numbers one through ten are: <em>abã</em>, <em>biyã</em>, <em>kanokũ</em>, <em>bibiti</em>, <em>abaka</em>, <em>abati</em>, <em>karaxi</em>, <em>kamiti</em>, <em>bitiya</em>, <em>abãraka</em>.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <!-- DEEP DIVE -->
    <article id="gram-tobe" class="card accent" style="margin-top: 28px;">
      <span class="pill">Deep dive</span>
      <h3 style="margin-top: 10px;">Two ways to say “I am”</h3>
      <p>One of the nicest things about Ahiyaniwa is how flexible its stative clauses are. You get <strong>two equally valid paths</strong> for linking a subject to an adjective, and you can choose whichever one fits the moment.</p>

      <div class="deep-dive-grid">
        <div class="deep-dive-box">
          <h4>The synthetic path, compact and traditional</h4>
          <p>Sandwich the adjective between the pronoun prefix and the stative suffix <em>ká</em>. This produces a single, tightly woven word and gives you that classic Arawakan rhythm.</p>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 0;"><em>Daxaliká</em> “I am happy.” <em>Busiwatuká</em> “You are tired.” <em>L'anuyuká</em> “He is sick.” <em>L'ahiyatiká teketa</em> “He is very talkative.”</p>
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-box">
          <h4>The analytic path, clear and easy to process</h4>
          <p>Use the standalone copula <em>daká, buká, toká</em> and then place the unmodified adjective right after it. This makes it easy to drop in modifiers like <em>teketa</em> “very.”</p>
          <p style="margin-bottom: 0;"><em>Daká xali</em> “I am happy.” <em>Buká hamuwei</em> “You are hungry.” <em>Waká tata</em> “We are strong.” <em>Toká teketa anako</em> “It is very beautiful.”</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="small" style="margin-bottom: 0;">Both are completely grammatical. Many speakers reach for the analytic form when they are building fluency or emphasizing a modifier, and the synthetic form in poetry, storytelling, or whenever they want that flowing Arawakan feel. Try mixing both into your practice and see which one fits your voice.</p>
    </article>

    <!-- SUFFIXES & POSTPOSITIONS -->
    <div id="gram-suffixes" class="section-head" style="margin-top: 56px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
      <span class="pill coral">Suffixes &amp; Postpositions</span>
      <h2>Noun and verb suffixes</h2>
      <p>Ahiyaniwa builds most of its vocabulary by adding meaningful pieces to a root. Some suffixes attach to nouns, others attach to verbs, and a few work with both. Here is a tour of the most useful ones, each with at least one example so you can see how it behaves in a real word.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="filter-bar" id="suffixFilters" aria-label="Filter affixes">
      <button class="filter-pill active" data-suffix-filter="all">All (26)</button>
      <button class="filter-pill" data-suffix-filter="noun">Noun Suffixes (10)</button>
      <button class="filter-pill" data-suffix-filter="verb">Verb Suffixes &amp; Prefixes (8)</button>
      <button class="filter-pill" data-suffix-filter="postposition">Example Postpositions (8)</button>
    </div>

    <div class="suffix-group" data-suffix-group="noun">
      <div class="sub-section-head" style="margin-top: 12px;">
        <h3>Noun suffixes</h3>
        <span class="sub-section-tag">10 morphemes</span>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-2">
        <article class="suffix-card" data-cat="noun">
          <div class="suffix-head">
            <span class="suffix-form">no</span>
            <span class="suffix-type">animate / human plural</span>
          </div>
          <div class="suffix-gloss">Marks more than one person, animal, or living thing.</div>
          <p>Attach it to a human or animate noun to make it plural. You can skip it when a number word already shows the plural.</p>
          <div class="suffix-example"><em>kasike</em> “chief” → <em>kasikeno</em> “chiefs”<br><em>inaru</em> “woman” → <em>inaruno</em> “women”</div>
        </article>

        <article class="suffix-card" data-cat="noun">
          <div class="suffix-head">
            <span class="suffix-form">be</span>
            <span class="suffix-type">inanimate / general plural</span>
          </div>
          <div class="suffix-gloss">Marks more than one object, place, or general noun.</div>
          <p>Use it on inanimate and general nouns. It is the everyday plural for things rather than people.</p>
          <div class="suffix-example"><em>ara</em> “tree” → <em>arabe</em> “trees”<br><em>ana</em> “flower” → <em>anabe</em> “flowers”</div>
        </article>

        <article class="suffix-card" data-cat="noun">
          <div class="suffix-head">
            <span class="suffix-form">ti / tu</span>
            <span class="suffix-type">agent marker</span>
          </div>
          <div class="suffix-gloss">Turns a verb into the person who does the action. <strong>ti</strong> is masculine, <strong>tu</strong> is feminine.</div>
          <p>Attach it to a verb root to name the doer. It is one of the most productive suffixes in the language.</p>
          <div class="suffix-example"><em>ahiya</em> “speak” → <em>ahiyati</em> “male speaker,” <em>ahiyatu</em> “female speaker”<br><em>bori</em> “work” → <em>boriti</em> “male worker”</div>
        </article>

        <article class="suffix-card" data-cat="noun">
          <div class="suffix-head">
            <span class="suffix-form">ro / li</span>
            <span class="suffix-type">nominalizer</span>
          </div>
          <div class="suffix-gloss">Makes a noun out of a root. <strong>ro</strong> is feminine and <strong>li</strong> is masculine.</div>
          <p>Use it when the noun refers to a female person or a feminine thing.</p>
          <div class="suffix-example"><em>irahu</em> “young” → <em>irahuro</em> “girl”<br><em>irahu</em> “young” → <em>irahuli</em> “boy”</div>
        </article>

        <article class="suffix-card" data-cat="noun">
          <div class="suffix-head">
            <span class="suffix-form">to</span>
            <span class="suffix-type">nominalizer</span>
          </div>
          <div class="suffix-gloss">Turns a verb or adjective into a noun for the action or the thing itself.</div>
          <p>It is a general purpose nominalizer and works well for names of events, objects, or results.</p>
          <div class="suffix-example"><em>arei</em> “sing” → <em>areito</em> “ceremonial song and dance”<br><em>bori</em> “work” → <em>borito</em> “job”</div>
        </article>

        <article class="suffix-card" data-cat="noun">
          <div class="suffix-head">
            <span class="suffix-form">ya</span>
            <span class="suffix-type">essence / instrumental</span>
          </div>
          <div class="suffix-gloss">Names the inner essence of something, or the tool used to do it.</div>
          <p>It often appears on nouns that name a quality or a thing that embodies that quality.</p>
          <div class="suffix-example"><em>kabu</em> “tie” → <em>kabuya</em> “rope”<br><em>bori</em> “work” → <em>boriya</em> “peasant/commoner”</div>
        </article>

        <article class="suffix-card" data-cat="noun">
          <div class="suffix-head">
            <span class="suffix-form">hu</span>
            <span class="suffix-type">abstract nominalizer</span>
          </div>
          <div class="suffix-gloss">Turns a verb into the idea or abstract concept behind the action.</div>
          <p>Use it when you want to talk about an action as a thing, like “speech” from “speak.”</p>
          <div class="suffix-example"><em>ahiya</em> “speak” → <em>ahiyahu</em> “speech, the act of speaking”<br><em>eixu</em> “think” → <em>eixuhu</em> “thought”</div>
        </article>

        <article class="suffix-card" data-cat="noun">
          <div class="suffix-head">
            <span class="suffix-form">wa</span>
            <span class="suffix-type">adjectival</span>
          </div>
          <div class="suffix-gloss">Makes an adjective meaning “having the quality of” or “relating to” the root.</div>
          <p>Attach it to a noun when you want to describe something as being full of, made of, or like that noun.</p>
          <div class="suffix-example"><em>wehi</em> “sun” → <em>wehiwa</em> “sunny”<br><em>turei</em> “sky” → <em>tureiwa</em> “heavenly, celestial”</div>
        </article>

        <article class="suffix-card" data-cat="noun">
          <div class="suffix-head">
            <span class="suffix-form">el</span>
            <span class="suffix-type">patronymic</span>
          </div>
          <div class="suffix-gloss">Means “son of” or “descendant of,” used in names and kin terms.</div>
          <p>It appears mostly in personal names and lineage words. It is one of the rare suffixes that can end a word in a consonant.</p>
          <div class="suffix-example"><em>semi</em> “spirit” → <em>semi(y)el</em> “son of the spirit”<br><em>waroko</em> “our grandfather” → <em>warokoel</em> “descendant of our grandfather”</div>
        </article>

        <article class="suffix-card" data-cat="noun">
          <div class="suffix-head">
            <span class="suffix-form">ha</span>
            <span class="suffix-type">comparative</span>
          </div>
          <div class="suffix-gloss">Makes an adjective comparative, like “er” in English.</div>
          <p>Attach it to an adjective to say that something has more of that quality than something else.</p>
          <div class="suffix-example"><em>tuyu</em> “tall” → <em>tuyuha</em> “taller”<br><em>xoko</em> “small” → <em>xokoha</em> “smaller”</div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="suffix-group" data-suffix-group="verb">
      <div class="sub-section-head">
        <h3>Verb suffixes</h3>
        <span class="sub-section-tag">8 morphemes</span>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-2">
        <article class="suffix-card" data-cat="verb">
          <div class="suffix-head">
            <span class="suffix-form">ká</span>
            <span class="suffix-type">present / stative / habitual</span>
          </div>
          <div class="suffix-gloss">Marks an ongoing state or something done regularly. Also serves as the copula “am, is, are.”</div>
          <p>This is the most common verb suffix. It covers present tense, habitual actions, and states of being.</p>
          <div class="suffix-example"><em>d'ahiyaká</em> “I speak”<br><em>tuboriká</em> “she works”<br><em>daká</em> “I am”</div>
        </article>

        <article class="suffix-card" data-cat="verb">
          <div class="suffix-head">
            <span class="suffix-form">bá</span>
            <span class="suffix-type">past / perfective</span>
          </div>
          <div class="suffix-gloss">Marks a finished action in the past.</div>
          <p>Use it when the action is complete, whether it happened recently or long ago.</p>
          <div class="suffix-example"><em>b'ahiyabá</em> “you spoke”<br><em>n'arikēbá</em> “they saw”<br><em>dabá</em> “I was”</div>
        </article>

        <article class="suffix-card" data-cat="verb">
          <div class="suffix-head">
            <span class="suffix-form">bó</span>
            <span class="suffix-type">progressive</span>
          </div>
          <div class="suffix-gloss">Marks an action happening right now, like “ing” in English.</div>
          <p>It is the suffix you reach for when you want to say that something is actively in progress.</p>
          <div class="suffix-example"><em>d'ahiyabó</em> “I am speaking”<br><em>d'abokabó</em> “I am cooking”<br><em>w'areibó</em> “we are singing”</div>
        </article>

        <article class="suffix-card" data-cat="verb">
          <div class="suffix-head">
            <span class="suffix-form">há</span>
            <span class="suffix-type">future / irrealis</span>
          </div>
          <div class="suffix-gloss">Marks something that will happen, might happen, or is intended.</div>
          <p>Use it for future plans and hypothetical situations alike.</p>
          <div class="suffix-example"><em>d'ahiyahá</em> “I will speak”<br><em>w'okonahá</em> “we will travel”<br><em>dahá</em> “I will be”</div>
        </article>

        <article class="suffix-card" data-cat="verb">
          <div class="suffix-head">
            <span class="suffix-form">té</span>
            <span class="suffix-type">imperative</span>
          </div>
          <div class="suffix-gloss">Makes a command or a request.</div>
          <p>Attach it to the verb root after the subject prefix to tell someone to do something.</p>
          <div class="suffix-example"><em>b'ahiyaté!</em> “Speak!”<br><em>hukãmaté!</em> “You all listen!”<br><em>b'ataté!</em> “Drink!”</div>
        </article>

        <article class="suffix-card" data-cat="verb">
          <div class="suffix-head">
            <span class="suffix-form">ra</span>
            <span class="suffix-type">verbalizer</span>
          </div>
          <div class="suffix-gloss">Turns a noun or adjective into a verb meaning “to do” or “to make” that thing.</div>
          <p>It is the standard way to create a new verb from a noun. The resulting verb then takes normal tense suffixes.</p>
          <div class="suffix-example"><em>axula</em> “kiss” → <em>axulara</em> “to kiss”<br><em>kusiba</em> “ice” → <em>kusibara</em> “to freeze, to ice”</div>
        </article>

        <article class="suffix-card" data-cat="verb">
          <div class="suffix-head">
            <span class="suffix-form">a</span>
            <span class="suffix-type">become</span>
          </div>
          <div class="suffix-gloss">A prefix that means “to become” the quality named by the root.</div>
          <p>Strictly speaking this one goes at the front of the word, but it behaves like a derivational suffix in how it changes the meaning of a root.</p>
          <div class="suffix-example"><em>sanako</em> “stupid” → <em>asanako</em> “to become stupid”<br><em>xali</em> “happy” → <em>axali</em> “to become happy”</div>
        </article>

        <article class="suffix-card" data-cat="verb">
          <div class="suffix-head">
            <span class="suffix-form">ma</span>
            <span class="suffix-type">negative prefix</span>
          </div>
          <div class="suffix-gloss">Means “without” or “not,” and negates the word it attaches to.</div>
          <p>Like the “become” prefix, it goes at the front of the word rather than the end, but it belongs in this list because it is one of the most important meaning changing pieces in the language.</p>
          <div class="suffix-example"><em>xali</em> “happy” → <em>maxali</em> “unhappy”<br><em>kabu</em> “importance” → <em>makabu</em> “unimportant”<br><em>suruti</em> “right, correct” → <em>masuruti</em> “wrong”</div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="suffix-group" data-suffix-group="postposition">
      <div class="sub-section-head">
        <h3>Example Postpositions</h3>
        <span class="sub-section-tag">8 morphemes</span>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-2">
        <article class="suffix-card" data-cat="postposition">
          <div class="suffix-head">
            <span class="suffix-form">kowa</span>
            <span class="suffix-type">locative postposition</span>
          </div>
          <div class="suffix-gloss">Means “at,” “on,” or “at the edge of.”</div>
          <p>Attach it to a place noun to say where something happens. It also appears in compound place names.</p>
          <div class="suffix-example"><em>burẽ</em> “griddle” → <em>kaburẽkowa</em> “at the griddle”<br><em>beyabu</em> “beach” → <em>kabeyabukowa</em> “at the beach”</div>
        </article>

        <article class="suffix-card" data-cat="postposition">
          <div class="suffix-head">
            <span class="suffix-form">mũ</span>
            <span class="suffix-type">dative postposition</span>
          </div>
          <div class="suffix-gloss">Means “to” or “for.”</div>
          <p>Use it to mark the person or thing that receives the action or benefits from it.</p>
          <div class="suffix-example"><em>de</em> “me” → <em>demũ</em> “to me, for me”<br><em>bohiyo</em> “house” → <em>kabohiyomũ</em> “to the house”</div>
        </article>

        <article class="suffix-card" data-cat="postposition">
          <div class="suffix-head">
            <span class="suffix-form">oriya</span>
            <span class="suffix-type">ablative / genitive postposition</span>
          </div>
          <div class="suffix-gloss">Means “from,” “out of,” or “of.”</div>
          <p>Use it to mark the source of something or the material it is made from.</p>
          <div class="suffix-example"><em>siba</em> “stone” → <em>sib'oriya</em> “of stone”<br><em>hiwera</em> “gourd” → <em>kahiwer'oriya</em> “from the gourd”</div>
        </article>

        <article class="suffix-card" data-cat="postposition">
          <div class="suffix-head">
            <span class="suffix-form">oma</span>
            <span class="suffix-type">associative postposition</span>
          </div>
          <div class="suffix-gloss">Means “with” in the sense of “in the company of.”</div>
          <p>Use it when you are together with a person or animal, rather than using a tool.</p>
          <div class="suffix-example"><em>wo</em> “us” → <em>w'oma</em> “with us”<br><em>awō</em> “dog” → <em>d'awõmoma</em> “with my dog”</div>
        </article>

        <article class="suffix-card" data-cat="postposition">
          <div class="suffix-head">
            <span class="suffix-form">abo</span>
            <span class="suffix-type">instrumental postposition</span>
          </div>
          <div class="suffix-gloss">Means “with” in the sense of “using” or “by means of.”</div>
          <p>Use it when the noun is the tool or material used to do something. It often needs an epenthetic <em>m</em> or <em>n</em> before it.</p>
          <div class="suffix-example"><em>kawõna</em> “gold” → <em>kawõn'abo</em> “with gold, gold containing”</div>
        </article>

        <article class="suffix-card" data-cat="postposition">
          <div class="suffix-head">
            <span class="suffix-form">yo</span>
            <span class="suffix-type">directional postposition</span>
          </div>
          <div class="suffix-gloss">Means “toward” or “in the direction of.”</div>
          <p>Attach it to a place noun to show motion in that direction.</p>
          <div class="suffix-example"><em>arabuko</em> “forest” → <em>karabukoyo</em> “toward the forest”<br><em>ikẽ</em> “land” → <em>kikẽyo</em> “toward the land”</div>
        </article>

        <article class="suffix-card" data-cat="postposition">
          <div class="suffix-head">
            <span class="suffix-form">oloko</span>
            <span class="suffix-type">inessive postposition</span>
          </div>
          <div class="suffix-gloss">Means “inside” or “within.”</div>
          <p>Use it to say that something is contained within a place or object.</p>
          <div class="suffix-example"><em>bohiyo</em> “house” → <em>kabohiy'oloko</em> “inside the house”<br><em>arabuko</em> “forest” → <em>karabuk'oloko</em> “inside the forest”</div>
        </article>

        <article class="suffix-card" data-cat="postposition">
          <div class="suffix-head">
            <span class="suffix-form">kei</span>
            <span class="suffix-type">similative postposition</span>
          </div>
          <div class="suffix-gloss">Means “like” or “as.”</div>
          <p>Use it to compare one thing to another without saying they are the same.</p>
          <div class="suffix-example"><em>semi</em> “spirit” → <em>semikei</em> “like a spirit”<br><em>awara</em> “bird” → <em>awarakei</em> “like a bird”</div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="note">
      <strong>A note on building words.</strong> You can stack suffixes in a single word as long as the meaning stays clear. A form like <em>ahiya</em> “speak” can grow into <em>ahiyati</em> “speaker,” then <em>ahiyatihu</em> “the quality of being a speaker,” and so on. The language rewards patience and pattern spotting, and the more you read, the more natural the combinations will feel.
    </div>
  </section>

