    <div class="example-group" data-ex-group="greetings">
      <div class="sub-section-head">
        <h3>Greetings and introductions</h3>
        <span class="sub-section-tag">Glossed sentences</span>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-2">
        <article class="example-card">
          <div class="ta">Miyari kena maburika! Toká d'iri Maria, tuwei toká b'iri?</div>
          <div class="en">Hello and welcome! My name is Maria, what is your name?</div>
          <div class="breakdown">Miyari “hello,” kena “and,” maburika “welcome.” Toká “it is” plus d'iri “my name.” Tuwei “what,” toká “it is,” b'iri “your name.”</div>
        </article>
        <article class="example-card">
          <div class="ta">Taiwei! Halika buká ukairã?</div>
          <div class="en">Good morning! How are you today?</div>
          <div class="breakdown">Taiwei “good morning,” halika “how,” buká “you are,” ukairã “today.”</div>
        </article>
        <article class="example-card">
          <div class="ta">Daká teketa xali, hahõ! Kena d'ahiyabó ahiyaniwa taino b'oma.</div>
          <div class="en">I am very happy, thank you! And I am speaking the Taíno language with you.</div>
          <div class="breakdown">Daká “I am,” teketa “very,” xali “happy,” hahõ “thank you.” D'ahiyabó “I am speaking,” ahiyaniwa taino “Taíno language,” b'oma “with you.”</div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="example-group" data-ex-group="food">
      <div class="sub-section-head">
        <h3>Cooking, food, and hospitality</h3>
        <span class="sub-section-tag">Glossed sentences</span>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-2">
        <article class="example-card">
          <div class="ta">Hamuwei buká? D'abokabó kasabe kaburẽkowa.</div>
          <div class="en">Are you hungry? I am cooking cassava bread on the griddle.</div>
          <div class="breakdown">Hamuwei “hungry,” buká “you are.” D'abokabó “I am cooking,” kasabe “cassava bread,” kaburẽkowa “on the griddle.”</div>
        </article>
        <article class="example-card">
          <div class="ta">W'eikahá batata, hime, kena semiku wayaba dabohiy'oloko.</div>
          <div class="en">We will eat sweet potato, fish, and sweet guava inside my house.</div>
          <div class="breakdown">W'eikahá “we will eat,” batata “sweet potato,” hime “fish,” kena “and,” semiku wayaba “sweet guava,” dabohiy'oloko “inside my house.”</div>
        </article>
        <article class="example-card">
          <div class="ta">Ikiwa, b'ataté kurula ni kahiwer'oriya!</div>
          <div class="en">Please, drink cold water from the gourd!</div>
          <div class="breakdown">Ikiwa “please,” b'ataté “drink,” kurula ni “cold water,” kahiwer'oriya “from the gourd.”</div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="example-group" data-ex-group="nature">
      <div class="sub-section-head">
        <h3>Nature, travel, and village life</h3>
        <span class="sub-section-tag">Glossed sentences</span>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-2">
        <article class="example-card">
          <div class="ta">Harukarã, w'okonahá karabukoyo d'awõmoma.</div>
          <div class="en">Tomorrow, we will travel toward the forest with my dog.</div>
          <div class="breakdown">Harukarã “tomorrow,” w'okonahá “we will travel,” karabukoyo “toward the forest,” d'awõmoma “with my dog.”</div>
        </article>
        <article class="example-card">
          <div class="ta">N'arikẽbá ababe hikoteya kena aba manati kabeyabukowa.</div>
          <div class="en">They saw some sea turtles and a manatee at the beach.</div>
          <div class="breakdown">N'arikẽbá “they saw,” ababe hikoteya “some sea turtles,” kena “and,” aba manati “a manatee,” kabeyabukowa “at the beach.”</div>
        </article>
        <article class="example-card">
          <div class="ta">T'areiká bi sũsũ kawayabakowa, kena toká teketa anako.</div>
          <div class="en">The small hummingbird sings at the guava tree, and it is very beautiful.</div>
          <div class="breakdown">T'areiká “it sings,” bi sũsũ “small hummingbird,” kawayabakowa “at the guava tree,” kena “and,” toká “it is,” teketa “very,” anako “beautiful.”</div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="example-group" data-ex-group="stories">
      <div class="sub-section-head">
        <h3>Storytelling, music, and evening gatherings</h3>
        <span class="sub-section-tag">Glossed sentences</span>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-2">
